**This case uses ONE link to EACH partner as the two equal-cost next hops**, not the swi_a pair:

  swi_d port1.0.4  vlan41 10.0.41.1/30  -> next hop **10.0.41.2** (swi_a)
  swi_d port1.0.16 vlan61 10.0.61.1/30  -> next hop **10.0.61.2** (swi_b)

🔴 **WHY NOT THE swi_a PAIR (port1.0.4 + port1.0.14), which the triage recommended.**  RSTP here
is **single-instance**.  Two parallel links between the same pair of bridges are a loop that
RSTP must break at one end whichever bridge is root, and a Discarding port is blocked for every
VLAN, so putting the two links in different VLANs does not free them.  With the DUT made root
for this group, swi_a blocks one of its two DUT-facing ports; with swi_b root (the bench's
natural state) the DUT blocked both.  **There is no configuration of single-instance RSTP in
which both links of a parallel pair forward.**  The queue's own group-B note sanctions the
alternative -- "ECMP uses the DUT's two links to one partner, **or one link to each**" -- and one
link to each partner is a loop-free star, so both legs forward with spanning tree left enabled
and doing its job.  Full reasoning and the measured before/after STP in the queue's
"## Group B/C/D shared L3 underlay and restore" section and in T43855/work/run1.log §5.

STP verified before this case and re-read before EVERY counted leg:
