  port1.0.16  0000:e01aead47043  8398  Designated  Forwarding


3. CASE TEXT -- NONE EXISTS
----------------------------
As for T43855: no authored step text or expected result for T43861 in AWPTCM or ck.db.  Grounded
in and cited against:
  (a) **TPS row 37 "ECMP (Equal-cost Multi-Path) routing" -- priority 1, `Yes`, note empty.**
  (b) **Route Selection Feature Overview and Configuration Guide, C613-22029-00 REV G**,
      "Equal Cost Multipath Routing" pp.10-11.  Quoted verbatim where a limb tests a claim:
      > "The software determines that two or more routes are equal cost if they have the same
      >  destination IP address and mask, Administrative Distance, Metric, and protocol. When
      >  the software learns such multiple routes, it puts them in an ECMP route group. When it
      >  sends traffic to that destination, it distributes the traffic across all routes in the
      >  group."
      > "On AlliedWare Plus switches, ECMP calculations are done on a per-packet basis. This
      >  means that the packets in a flow may take different paths."
      > "By default, each equal-cost route group can contain four routes. You can change this
      >  setting by using the command: awplus(config)# maximum-paths <1-8>"
      > "To disable ECMP, set the maximum paths value to one."

**The evidence standard this case is held to** (dispatch, STANDING-ORDERS §7): ECMP may NOT be
graded on `show ip route` or on a ping.  It needs many flows with varying 5-tuples, counted
**per egress port**, with `maximum-paths 1` as the control that must collapse everything onto a
single port.  Every verdict below rests on per-port packet counters, never on a route read-back.


4. THE INSTRUMENT
------------------
**`tools/ecmpflows.py` -- new in this case, catalogued in `tools/README.md`.**  Nothing in
`tools/` varied all four hash-input fields: `vsend.py` sends one burst of one kind, `mkpcap.py`
varies destination address and source port only, `linerate.py` is a single stream.  The new tool
builds N flows differing in source IP, destination IP, source port and destination port (any
subset, chosen with `--vary`), sends M frames of each from a host NIC at L2 with an explicit
next-hop MAC, captures on the sending NIC, and reports both what it sent and what the capture
holds.  Every payload carries `CKECMP-<tag>`, and it **exits 7 if the capture holds fewer of its
own marked frames than it sent**, so a dead instrument can never be read as a result.

  SELF-TEST, before any measurement (20:46 NZDT):
    sudo python3.8 tools/ecmpflows.py --tag SELFTEST --out /tmp/ckB/T43861 --iface eth3 \
      --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --flows 4 --per-flow 2 --proto udp --vary src,sport,dport
    -> 4 distinct flows printed, "sent 8 frame(s)", "cap SELFTEST-eth3.pcap: 8 frame(s), 8 of
       them ours", each flow x2.  Instrument proven before it was trusted.
  Every measured leg below also reported "N of them ours" equal to what it sent; none exited 7.

**PER-EGRESS-PORT COUNTERS are the primary instrument.**  Read on the devices themselves with
`show interface <port>`, before and after each leg, and differenced:
  swi_d port1.0.4  "output packets"  -- frames the DUT sent toward swi_a
  swi_d port1.0.16 "output packets"  -- frames the DUT sent toward swi_b
  swi_a port1.0.4  "input packets"   -- frames swi_a actually received (independent witness)
  swi_b port1.0.16 "input packets"   -- frames swi_b actually received (independent witness)
Scripts on the box: /tmp/ckB/ctrs.sh (all four) and /tmp/ckB/ctrd.sh (the DUT's two, fast).

**BACKGROUND DRIFT MEASURED FIRST, so the noise is a number and not a hope** (20:45:58 ->
20:46:21, 23 s, nothing sent):
  swi_d port1.0.4  output  731 -> 767   = **+36**
