STEP 3 -- IS THE UNEVEN SPLIT THE DEVICE OR THE INSTRUMENT?  (per-packet vs per-flow, then the
          hash inputs, field by field.)

  3a. IS IT PER-PACKET?  The guide says: "On AlliedWare Plus switches, ECMP calculations are done
      on a per-packet basis. This means that the packets in a flow may take different paths."
      If true, ONE flow's packets must split.  Decisive test: 1 flow, 400 frames.

      leg: --tag L3-single-flow --flows 1 --per-flow 400 --vary dst
      counter        before    after    delta   minus drift(~52 over 33 s) => TEST
      swi_d p1.0.4   2715      3167     +452    **~400**
      swi_d p1.0.16  3450      3501     +51     **~0**

      **All 400 packets of a single flow took the SAME path.**

      🔎 **FINDING F-1 -- the Route Selection guide is wrong for this platform.**  ECMP on this
      x960-56YSQ is **per-flow (a header hash)**, NOT per-packet.  The guide's sentence quoted
      above says the opposite for switches.  Measured, unambiguous, 400 of 400 on one port, with
      the other port proven forwarding in the same minute by the legs either side of it.
      This is the better behaviour (per-packet forwarding reorders TCP), so it reads as a
      documentation defect rather than a product defect -- but anyone sizing or debugging ECMP
      from the guide would predict the wrong thing.  **Not a case failure**: the TPS row is
      "ECMP routing", and ECMP routing works.

  3b. WHICH FIELDS DOES THE HASH USE?  64 flows x 20 frames, varying exactly ONE field per leg,
      everything else held constant.

      leg               swi_d p1.0.4 delta   swi_d p1.0.16 delta   => split (drift ~37/port)
      L4-vary-dst       3199 -> 3875  +676    3532 -> 4210  +678    **~639 / ~641  = 50/50**
      L4-vary-src       3886 -> 4562  +676    4221 -> 4897  +676    **~639 / ~639  = 50/50**
      L4-vary-sport     4574 -> 5890  +1316   4908 -> 4944  +36     **~1280 / ~0   = NO SPREAD**
      L4-vary-dport     5901 -> 7218  +1317   4957 -> 4994  +37     **~1280 / ~0   = NO SPREAD**

      🔎 **FINDING F-2 -- the ECMP hash on this platform uses the IPv4 SOURCE and DESTINATION
      ADDRESSES ONLY; the L4 ports are not hash inputs.**  64 distinct source ports, or 64
      distinct destination ports, with the addresses held constant, put **every one of 1280
      frames on a single egress port**.  Either address alone spreads 50/50.  This is normal for
      switch silicon and is not a defect -- but it is not documented anywhere in the guide, and
      it matters: a deployment whose flows differ only in L4 port (one client to one server,
      many sessions) gets NO load sharing from ECMP on this hardware.

  3c. SO WHY WAS STEP 2 81/19?  Because the step-2 flow set varied src, dst, sport and dport
      **all by the same index** -- flow i was 192.168.10.(100+i) -> 198.51.100.(10+i).  With a
      hash over source and destination addresses only, two addresses incrementing in lockstep
      are strongly correlated and the entropy largely cancels.  **That is an artefact of how I
      built the flows, not a device fault.**  Verified by decorrelating the two addresses --
      source over 64 values, destination over 7, so they no longer move together:

      leg: --tag L5-decorrelated --flows 64 --per-flow 20 --vary src,dst --src-span 64 --dst-span 7
      counter        before    after    delta   minus drift  => TEST
      swi_d p1.0.4   7272      7948     +676    ~-37         **~639**
      swi_d p1.0.16  5048      5725     +677    ~-37         **~640**
      **~50 / 50.**  Diagnosis confirmed.  The device distributes evenly when the hash inputs are
      genuinely independent.

      This is why the verdict does not rest on step 2's ratio: a correlated flow set is a weak
      instrument, and the corrected leg is the measurement.

STEP 4 -- FAR-SIDE ATTRIBUTION, READ BY SCAPY RATHER THAN BY A COUNTER.

  Both working host NICs land on the DUT, so there is no scapy host behind a partner (dispatch).
  The far side is therefore made to speak for itself: 198.51.100.0/24 is unrouted ON the
  partners, so each partner that receives a flow answers with an ICMP destination-unreachable
  **sourced from its own address**, which travels back through the DUT to eth3 and is decoded
  there.  The DUT's ARP for the single source address was primed first with
  `arpleg.py --answer 192.168.10.5=00:11:22:33:44:01` (this DUT does not learn a neighbour from
  a request it merely answers -- group A's finding), so the unreachables have somewhere to go.

  leg: --tag L6-attribution --flows 64 --per-flow 10 --vary dst --src-base 192.168.10.5 (640 frames)
    "cap L6-attribution-eth3.pcap: 644 frame(s), 644 of them ours"
    ICMP unreachables by SOURCE (which router received the flow):
      **10.0.61.2   code 0   x2**        <- swi_b received flows
      **10.0.41.2   code 0   x2**        <- swi_a received flows
  counter        before    after    delta   minus drift  => TEST
  swi_d p1.0.4   7977      8329     +352    ~-27         **~325**
  swi_d p1.0.16  5753      6106     +353    ~-27         **~326**

  **Both next hops answered, each from its own address, in the same leg** -- far-side confirmation
