    python3 tools/rcdiff.py 61-swia-pre.out 61-swia-post.out  -> NO OUTPUT (identical)
  (`rcdiff.py` PRINTS differences but EXITS 0; the empty output is the proof, never the exit code.)

  The shared B/C/D underlay is deliberately LEFT UP for groups C and D (STANDING-ORDERS §6).
  swi_a was never reloaded or power-cycled (RISK-1); it was read and configured over its console
  only, and its config is byte-identical to the start of this case.


7. WHAT THIS CASE DID NOT PROVE
--------------------------------
- **More than two paths.**  The bench has exactly two usable routed legs from the DUT under
  single-instance RSTP, so group sizes of 3-8 (`maximum-paths <1-8>`) were NOT exercised, and
  neither was the guide's claim that the DEFAULT group size is 4.  A third path needs either a
  third partner or spanning tree disabled on a parallel pair -- a bench change, not a test
  change.  **NOT MEASURED**, not assumed.
- **`weight <1-255>`** (weighted lottery mode).  `show ip route` prints `weight 1` on every
  static route on this switch, but the guide scopes the feature to firewalls and VPN routers.
  Whether the parameter is accepted and whether it biases the split on an x960 is **NOT
  MEASURED**; it would be the natural next experiment.
- **ECMP for dynamically learnt routes** (OSPF/RIP/BGP equal-cost paths) -- this case covers
  static routes only.  Groups C and D could extend it.
- **Hash inputs beyond the IPv4 5-tuple**: IPv6, MAC addresses, VLAN id, ingress port and
  protocol number were not varied.  F-2 states what WAS measured (source and destination IPv4
  addresses are inputs; L4 source and destination ports are not), and claims nothing more.
- **Re-hash on path change**: whether existing flows are redistributed when a path is added or
  removed was not measured as a continuity question (no loss measurement across the change).


