RUN END: 2026-10-15 21:06 NZDT (2026-10-15 08:06 UTC)

VERDICT: PASS -- ECMP on the x960-56YSQ distributes traffic over two equal-cost static next hops
in the DATAPLANE, counted per egress port on four independent counters across three devices: one
route puts 1920/1920 frames on one port, adding the second splits them, and `maximum-paths 1`
collapses the identical flow set back onto a single port (1280/0) as the guide says it must.
Removing a next hop returns all traffic to the survivor, and the configuration survives a real
reboot.  Three findings recorded, none a failure of the feature:
  F-1  ECMP here is PER-FLOW, not per-packet -- 400 packets of one flow all took one path.  The
       Route Selection guide states the opposite for AlliedWare Plus switches.
  F-2  the hash uses the IPv4 source and destination ADDRESSES only; 64 distinct source ports,
       or 64 distinct destination ports, produce NO spread at all.
  F-3  an ECMP next hop whose ARP is unresolved is silently absent from the dataplane while
       `show ip route database` still marks it `*>` -- seen after the reboot, where 100% of
       traffic took the one resolved path until the other next hop was pinged.
One instrument artefact was found and corrected inside the case: the first ECMP leg varied all
four 5-tuple fields by the same index, which correlates the two addresses the hash actually uses
and gave a misleading 81/19 split; decorrelating them restored 50/50.  The verdict rests on the
corrected legs.
